---

<ArticleLayout
    title={title}
    description={description}
    authorSlug={authorSlug}
    pubDate={pubDate}
    readingTime={readingTime}
    image={image}
    imageAlt={imageAlt}
    category="Communication"
    categorySlug="guides"
    subcategorySlug="communication"
    headings={headings}
>
  <!-- Introduction -->
  <h2 id="introduction">Email Is Still King Sometimes</h2>
  <p>
    In a world of <a href="/blog/best-team-communication-tools">Slack and Teams</a> and endless notification badges
    email might seem old-fashioned. But for remote teams
    email remains essential. It's the universal communication channel that works across organizations. Persists as a
    record. Doesn't demand instant attention.
  </p>
  <p>
    The challenge isn't whether to use email. It's using it well. Poor email habits waste time and create
    misunderstandings and clog inboxes. Good email communication respects recipients' time and communicates clearly and
    uses the medium appropriately.
  </p>
  <p>
    This covers email etiquette specifically for remote teams where written communication carries extra weight.
  </p>

  <!-- Email vs Slack -->
  <h2 id="email-vs-slack">When to Email vs When to Slack</h2>
  <p>
    Choosing the right channel is the first decision. Get it wrong and your message either gets lost in the noise or
    feels unnecessarily formal.
  </p>

  <h3>Use Email For</h3>
  <p>
    External communication with clients and vendors and partners. Formal documentation where decisions need a permanent
    record. Long-form content like detailed explanations and proposals and reports. Cross-organizational stuff when
    recipients use different tools. Sensitive topics when you need to compose carefully. Non-urgent matters when a
    response within twenty-four to forty-eight hours is fine.
  </p>

  <h3>Use Slack or Teams For</h3>
  <p>
    Quick questions that need brief answers. Internal updates and status updates and FYIs and announcements. Casual
    conversation and team chat and social interaction. Time-sensitive coordination when you need a response soon.
    <a href="/blog/async-communication-remote-teams">Collaborative discussion</a> where back-and-forth would clutter
    email.
  </p>

  <h3>The Decision Framework</h3>
  <p>
    Is this person outside my organization? Email. Does this need to be a formal record? Email. Is this longer than
    three paragraphs? Email or a doc. Do I need a response in the next few hours? Slack. Will this require
    back-and-forth? Slack or a meeting.
  </p>

  <!-- Subject Lines -->
  <h2 id="subject-lines">Subject Line Best Practices</h2>
  <p>
    Your subject line determines whether the email gets opened and when it gets read and whether it can be found later.
  </p>

  <h3>Be Specific and Descriptive</h3>
  <p>
    Vague subject lines force recipients to open the email to understand what it's about.
  </p>
  <p>
    Bad: Quick question. Good: Q: Approval needed for Q2 marketing budget.<br/>
    Bad: Update. Good: Project Alpha status: On track for March fifteenth launch.<br/>
    Bad: FYI. Good: FYI: New PTO policy effective January first.
  </p>

  <h3>Use Prefixes for Quick Scanning</h3>
  <p>
    Standardized prefixes help recipients prioritize. ACTION REQUIRED means recipient needs to do something. DECISION
    NEEDED means input required for a choice. FYI means informational and no response expected. URGENT means
    time-sensitive but use sparingly. EOM means end of message where the subject line is the full message.
  </p>

  <h3>Keep It Searchable</h3>
  <p>
    Include keywords someone might search for later. Project names. Client names.key dates. Document types.
  </p>

  <!-- Clear Writing -->
  <h2 id="clear-writing">Clear and Concise Writing</h2>
  <p>
    In remote work you can't rely on tone of voice or body language. Your writing carries the full communication burden.
  </p>

  <h3>Front-Load the Key Point</h3>
  <p>
    Busy people skim. Put the most important information first. What you need from them. The decision or question. The
    deadline. Context and background come after. Readers can dive deeper if needed.
  </p>

  <h3>Use Formatting for Readability</h3>
  <p>
    Short paragraphs of two to three sentences max. Bullet points for lists and options and action items. Bold text to
    highlight key information. Numbered lists for sequential steps or priorities.
  </p>

  <h3>One Topic Per Email</h3>
  <p>
    Emails with multiple unrelated topics get partially addressed. If you need to cover multiple things either send
    separate emails or clearly number each topic with its own action required.
  </p>

  <h3>Be Explicit About the Ask</h3>
  <p>
    Bad: Thoughts? Good: Please review and reply with approval or concerns by Friday.<br/>
    Bad: Let me know. Good: Please confirm which option you prefer A or B.
  </p>

  <h3>Read Before Sending</h3>
  <p>
    Quick review checklist. Is the subject line accurate and specific? Is the ask clear in the first paragraph? Are
    there any ambiguous statements? Have I included all necessary context? Is the tone appropriate?
  </p>

  <!-- Response Times -->
  <h2 id="response-times">Response Time Expectations</h2>
  <p>
    Unclear expectations around response time create anxiety for senders and pressure for recipients.
  </p>

  <h3>Set Team Norms</h3>
  <p>
    Agree on standard response times. Internal emails within twenty-four business hours. External and client emails
    within twenty-four hours. CC'd emails read within twenty-four hours and respond only if valuable. Urgent matters
    define what urgent means and use different channels.
  </p>

  <h3>Acknowledge Receipt</h3>
  <p>
    If you can't respond fully right away send a quick acknowledgment. Thanks for sending this. I'll review and get back
    to you by date. This sets expectations and prevents follow-up inquiries.
  </p>

  <h3>Use Deadline Language</h3>
  <p>
    When you need a response by a specific time say so clearly. Please respond by Thursday at five pm ET so I can
    finalize the proposal. I need to make a decision by Friday and any input before then is appreciated. Be explicit
    about <a href="/blog/managing-time-zones-remote-teams">time zones</a> when working with distributed teams.
  </p>

  <!-- CC and BCC -->
  <h2 id="cc-bcc">CC and BCC Guidelines</h2>
  <p>
    Misuse of CC and BCC creates noise and erodes trust.
  </p>

  <h3>When to CC</h3>
  <p>
    Someone needs to be informed but isn't the primary recipient. You're documenting that someone has been looped in. A
    manager or stakeholder needs visibility.
  </p>

  <h3>When NOT to CC</h3>
  <p>
    To CYA cover yourself because this signals distrust. When the person doesn't need to know. To apply pressure by
    involving someone's boss.
  </p>

  <h3>When to BCC</h3>
  <p>
    Large distribution lists where recipients don't need to see each other. Protecting email addresses in external
    communications. Forwarding yourself a copy for records.
  </p>

  <h3>When NOT to BCC</h3>
  <p>
    To secretly loop someone in on a conversation. This destroys trust if discovered. To hide who else received the
    message when that matters.
  </p>

  <!-- Sensitive Topics -->
  <h2 id="sensitive-topics">Handling Sensitive Topics</h2>
  <p>
    Some topics require extra care in email form.
  </p>

  <h3>Consider If Email Is Right</h3>
  <p>
    For highly sensitive topics like performance issues and conflicts and major bad news consider whether a video call
    would be better. Email lacks tone cues and doesn't allow for real-time response to reactions.
  </p>

  <h3>If You Must Email</h3>
  <p>
    Take time to compose. Write and save as draft and review later. Be factual. Stick to observations not
    interpretations. Avoid emotion. Angry emails almost always make things worse. Offer to discuss. I'd be happy to talk
    through this if helpful. Check recipients carefully. Ensure you're sending to the right person.
  </p>

  <h3>The Twenty-Four Hour Rule</h3>
  <p>
    For any email you're writing while frustrated or upset wait twenty-four hours before sending. You'll almost always
    edit it significantly or choose a different approach.
  </p>

  <!-- Signatures -->
  <h2 id="signatures">Email Signatures for Remote Workers</h2>
  <p>
    Your signature is part of your professional presence.
  </p>

  <h3>Essential Elements</h3>
  <p>
    Full name.title or role. Company name. Best contact method if not email.
  </p>

  <h3>Optional Elements</h3>
  <p>
    Phone number if you want calls.time zone which is helpful for remote teams. LinkedIn profile. Pronouns. Working
    hours note.
  </p>

  <h3>Example Signature</h3>
  <pre>
Best,
Sarah Chen

Sarah Chen
Product Manager | FloatJet
Based in PST (UTC-8)
calendly. Com/sarah-chen
  </pre>

  <h3>What to Avoid</h3>
  <p>
    Inspirational quotes are unprofessional in most contexts. Large images often break in email clients. Too many links
    or social profiles. Legal disclaimers unless legally required.
  </p>

  <!-- Templates -->
  <h2 id="templates">Templates for Common Scenarios</h2>

  <h3>Requesting Information</h3>
  <pre>
Subject: [ACTION REQUIRED] Budget numbers for Q2 planning

Hi [Name],

I'm preparing the Q2 marketing plan and need the following from you:
1. Final Q1 spend by category
2. Any committed Q2 expenses
3. New budget requests with justification

Could you send this by [date]? Let me know if you have questions.

Thanks,
[Your name]
  </pre>

  <h3>Following Up</h3>
  <pre>
Subject: Re: [Original subject] - following up

Hi [Name],

Following up on my email from [date] below. I'm working on
[context] and need your input to move forward.

Could you get back to me by [date]? If you need more time
or have questions, just let me know.

Thanks,
[Your name]
  </pre>

  <h3>Sharing a Decision</h3>
  <pre>
Subject: Decision: [Topic] - moving forward with Option B

Hi team,

After reviewing input from [names], we've decided to go with
Option B for [topic].

Key reasons:
- [Reason 1]
- [Reason 2]
- [Reason 3]

Next steps:
- [Name] will [action] by [date]
- [Name] will [action] by [date]

Questions? <a href="/guides/slack-vs-teams-vs-discord">Reply here or ping me on Slack</a>.

[Your name]
  </pre>

  <h3>Out of Office Response</h3>
  <pre>
Thanks for your email. I'm out of office from [date] to [date]
with limited access to email.

For urgent matters, please contact [name] at [email].

I'll respond to your message when I return.
  </pre>

  <!-- FAQ Section -->
  <FAQSection faqs={faqs}/>

  <h2>Email as Professional Communication</h2>
  <p>
    Email remains a cornerstone of professional communication especially for remote teams. Done well it respects
    recipients' time and communicates clearly and creates useful documentation.
  </p>
  <p>
    Key principles. Choose email when it's the right channel not out of habit. Write subject lines that inform and
    enable prioritization. Front-load your ask and make action items explicit. Set and respect response time
    expectations. Use CC thoughtfully and BCC sparingly. Pause before sending emotional emails.
  </p>
  <p>
    Your emails represent you professionally. Take the extra minute to make them clear and concise and respectful of the
    reader's time.
  </p>

  <!-- Related Articles -->
  <Fragment slot="full-width">
    <RelatedArticles articles={relatedArticles}/>
  </Fragment>
</ArticleLayout>
